1
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:15,974
JAMIE: Fox fired Maria Bartiromo, Steve Ballmer got suspended by the NBA, and somehow the most surprising part is that institutions waited this long to stop tolerating their worst people.
  ↳ source: https://www.axios.com/2026/09/03/maria-bartiromo-departs-fox

2
00:00:15,974 --> 00:00:42,932
ALEX: Markets woke up feeling frisky today. S&P up one point zero six percent to seven thousand seven hundred forty-seven, Nasdaq up one point four to twenty-six thousand five hundred eighty-four. The real story is Nvidia up three point two one percent—Oracle matching it at three point thirteen—while Adobe tanked two point two percent on the CEO transition. VIX got crushed to fourteen point three two, down nearly six points. This is pure risk-on: AI winners printing, legacy software getting reminded that nobody wants to pay for Creative Cloud anymore.

3
00:00:42,932 --> 00:00:52,516
JAMIE: So Adobe's losing its CEO and the stock's like, 'Good riddance'—that's the opposite of a vote of confidence. Are we watching investors just openly admit they've been waiting for an exit?

4
00:00:52,516 --> 00:01:08,691
MAYA: It's like when your roommate moves out and you finally get to paint over the beige walls—the space was always fine, but everyone agrees it's better now. Nvidia and Oracle are basically the only two people at the party still getting drunker while Adobe's trying to figure out if it's time to leave.

5
00:01:08,691 --> 00:01:22,469
JAMIE: So Fox News has been running Bartiromo since the 2000s—she's not some rotating contributor, she's the network's face for financial coverage. What changed is Rupert and Lachlan finally looked at her on-air conduct over the last four years and decided the liability outweighed the ratings.

6
00:01:22,469 --> 00:01:37,745
ALEX: The timing is interesting though. She's been peddling election denial and January 6th conspiracy material for years. Fox News has a credibility problem with advertisers and younger audiences—not because of one host, but because the whole operation became a political operation pretending to be news. Cutting her loose is theater.

7
00:01:37,745 --> 00:01:53,620
MAYA: But here's the thing—she's been floating toward Trump world anyway. There's reporting she's been in talks with him, and Fox is basically saying if you're going to go full political, go. They want to rebuild some distance from the administration so they can call themselves a news organization again when it matters.

8
00:01:53,620 --> 00:02:03,804
JAMIE: So Fox fires her to look credible, she probably joins Trump's orbit anyway, and Fox gets to tell advertisers they're serious about journalism. Everyone wins except the audience who wanted actual financial news.

9
00:02:03,804 --> 00:02:10,394
ALEX: Adobe just named Anil Chakravarthy as the new CEO, replacing Shantanu Narayen. Stock dropped two point two percent on the announcement.

10
00:02:10,394 --> 00:02:20,278
JAMIE: Two point two percent down on a leadership change—that's not 'oh, we're excited about fresh vision,' that's 'thank God we finally have an exit ramp.' What does Chakravarthy bring that Narayen couldn't?

11
00:02:20,278 --> 00:02:33,757
ALEX: He's been at Adobe since two thousand thirteen, ran the Document Cloud division. The market's betting that someone who actually knows the business can stop the bleeding on the subscription model while the company figures out whether generative AI is a feature or a funeral.

12
00:02:33,757 --> 00:02:45,439
MAYA: Steve Ballmer just got suspended by the NBA for a podcast rant and a jumbotron scheme involving Kawhi Leonard's endorsement deal—the guy who made 'developers' a meme finally found a platform that could actually get him in trouble.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/989962/steve-ballmer-kawhi-leonard-pablo-torre-finds-out

13
00:02:45,439 --> 00:02:49,932
JAMIE: Suspended—as in, he can't sit courtside? What exactly did he say on the podcast?

14
00:02:49,932 --> 00:03:02,811
ALEX: He denied the Clippers were involved in a twenty-eight million dollar endorsement deal that looked structurally designed to launder money into Kawhi's pocket. Then the investigation found the jumbotron was basically running the same shell-game playbook. So his defense was the lie.

15
00:03:02,811 --> 00:03:09,701
MAYA: Zscaler beat earnings and raised guidance—cybersecurity stocks don't need to pretend AI is solving their problems because their problems are already real.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/09/03/zscaler-zs-q4-earnings-2026.html

16
00:03:09,701 --> 00:03:17,488
ALEX: CEO Jay Chaudhry said their agentic Zero Trust offering is gaining early momentum. This is conviction=real territory—the company's actually shipping revenue, not a concept deck.

17
00:03:17,488 --> 00:03:36,359
MAYA: Oura's filing for an IPO on the back of Americans' obsession with tracking sleep, steps, and heart rate—we've monetized anxiety about not sleeping enough. The company's raised one hundred ninety million dollars so far, and the pitch is that wearable data plus preventative healthcare is the future. Except we're all just wearing tiny lie detectors that tell us we're failing at rest.

18
00:03:36,359 --> 00:03:51,335
JAMIE: So Oura's business model is basically charging people a subscription to feel worse about themselves in real time. You wake up, check your sleep score, it's a forty-seven, and now your whole day is ruined before coffee. That's not preventative healthcare—that's a quantified self-harm device with a ring attached.

19
00:03:51,335 --> 00:03:57,925
MAYA: Fox axing Bartiromo is the first domino—Rupert finally realized his brand's lifespan depends on cutting anchors before they become the story.

20
00:03:57,925 --> 00:04:05,413
ALEX: When the liability walks out the door and the stock ticks up, you know the institution was pricing in the cost of keeping her.

21
00:04:05,413 --> 00:04:09,606
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
